-- VALUES DON'T STEP — proved from the prompt, inside the import that asked for it.
--
-- Step through this a line at a time. `:where`, `:show` and `:matches` between
-- the steps are the demo. Verified end to end 2026-11-15: the import completes,
-- `valueNoStepAuto` is declared, and `:trust` says `resting on nothing`.
--
-- WHAT IS REAL AND WHAT IS NOT, so the claim made out loud is the true one.
-- REAL: the `case s of { }` step. Nothing in `noStep` names `E-beta`, `E-app1`
-- or `E-app2`; elaboration picks the split's arms and discharges each by
-- no-confusion on its own. Also real: the import parks, hands you the prompt
-- mid-elaboration, and takes the proof you built there.
-- NOT REAL: the navigation. `V-absCase1`, `V-absCase`, `V-absMethod` and
-- `goal1` are addresses written into the rule, and `s` is named in a fenced
-- fragment. A version whose moves are several clauses of one rule does search
-- and does reach ∎ — and cannot be reached through a `yield`, which is
-- `~7grfx` in .jalivert/REPORT.md.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The surface program says: I am not proving this at the prompt of a file.
--
--   valueNoStepAuto = \ m u v s -> do {
--     yield "do induction-on v at the prompt" ;
--   }
--
-- So the import stops in the middle and hands you the wheel.

import examples.demo.handmade.Lambda
:where

-- The goal is `Empty`, with a value derivation `v` and a step derivation `s`
-- in scope. This is the interesting goal and nothing else had to be typed to
-- reach it.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FIRST BEAT: ask the general search what it has. `:matches` is what the engine
-- would try here — 58 rows, and the nullary ones a bare `step` can reach are
-- try-in-scope / fit-in-scope / attack / abandon / step.
--
-- `step` IS NOT PART OF THIS BEAT ANY MORE, AND IT IS NOT A STEP YOU CAN TAKE
-- AND UNDO. Measured 2026-11-24, after the in-scope rules (engine phases 339,
-- 340) shipped: `fit-in-scope` here fits the declaration that is ITSELF being
-- elaborated, so the goal becomes `refined = valueNoStepAuto m1 u1 v1 s1` with
-- four fresh holes — the theorem appealing to itself. `regret` answers *no
-- clause of regret applies here*, because the choice point is the rule's and
-- not the line's, and `:undo` rewinds the whole `import`. So there is no way
-- back to this goal inside the run. `~qs3dj` in .jalivert/REPORT.md is the
-- engine-side entry; until it is answered, show `:matches` and go on.

:matches

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SECOND BEAT: the specialised base is already in scope — `Lambda.thena`
-- consults it at the top, which is the design claim: you write a rule base for
-- your own problem and the file says so.

:bases

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THIRD BEAT: say the one thing you are willing to say, and the base does the
-- rest. One command, and the proof is complete and committed.

induction-on "v"

-- What it prints, and what each line is:
--
--   subgoals: V-absMethod                                  induction on v
--   subgoals: V-absCase                                     one case: V-abs
--   subgoals: E-betaMethod, E-app1Method, E-app2Method       case s of { }
--   no subgoals                                              E-beta refuted
--   no subgoals                                              E-app1 refuted
--   no subgoals                                              E-app2 refuted

:show

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- FOURTH BEAT: hand control back, and the import carries on to the end with
-- the proof you just built in it.

yield
:show valueNoStepAuto
:trust
